le's character the shower  footage  that as michelle were thinking the  shower's on  in one shower and then she checks the  other shower but then she finds the  footage on the tv i thought that was  really clever  and once she sees this footage this is  where we first see the killer's  masked face and the michael myers show  begins i thought it was  funny the scene where they're kind of  throwing this  rock to push taylor into the ocean  because they messed up on the original  throw of his body  but it dragged a little bit here for the  sake of humor that maybe  wasn't even necessary and that would be  my only critique in the debut  of franco's film is i think his pacing  is a little  wonky and can get a little more tighter  and pick up a little quicker  a lot of thrillers and horrors that come  out now like doing this thing where  halfway through in the movie things pick  up and that's fine you want to earn to  get to that  quicker pace and build up your  characters but i do think  still really slow up parts before that  and they gotta wind it up a little  earlier but that's me nitpicking and  that's not something that i think was  too detrimental to the film i do wish  having to wait for big payoff with the  kills you want clever kills in an  audience you've seen a lot of slashers  before you've seen people chasing people  down so you want the big payoff and the  actual killing  i only think we got one of those which  was with mina i thought it was  really cool how she ended up dying  running off the cliff  and threw the fog so it spoke volumes it  connected to her character well  and it was a certain karma to it that's  why i wish charlie who's the most  unlikable hero got the best death and it  was just him getting hit by a hammer  as the other two characters did as well  so that was a little  bland and could have been more creative  and there was a weird head-scratching  moment  that was just strange to me i did not  understand was when  josh actually gets through this door  with mina  and they're looking through the boxes  and josh just finds these pictures  and we as the audience don't see them  clearly we don't really know what he saw  there  i mean is like what are those and he's  like jesus you don't want to know  okay that's a weird line to throw in  there because we never get an answer on  that what was the point of that that was  strange and why wouldn't he tell mina  but worked effectively emotionally in  the scene was josh hugging her saying  he's a piece of crap to meena  and you feel for josh again because this  is a big moment mina could have been  honest with him about the affair and  maybe  made him feel a little better about  himself but she doesn't and he doesn't  know about the affair which is a vital  info to all this and he doesn't know  that he actually didn't kill  taylor so it works to serve that purpose  of this  theme about how actions of just two  people affect the whole group and bad  decisions  and that mina and charlie just keep  repeating these mistakes of lying so  after  the killer ends up killing the four main  players here  i like the creepy moment of him looking  in the mirror with his mask on and like  i said before my favorite moment was a  dog running into the killer  clearly franco did not want to show you  the face of the killer because he wants  to keep that eeriness  and truth to it that killers like this  that  just peeping tom people and  kill them from afar you never see their  face so he wants to keep that effect to  make you think about it  later on after watching the film so i  think that was actually a smart choice  might frustrate some viewers though  because in movies like this you kind of  want some kind of twist or explanation  of to who this killer was so it's a  tough sell but i understand it to work  with the theme of this movie  and franco clearly wants to show in this  last montage  when we're seeing him go to different  rental places the killer how easy it is  for