h comes in  you find out they're brothers and he's  the one actually dating her so franco  does a good job of  kind of keeping you off guard about  what's really going on with this  weird kind of dynamic between these two  brothers and this girl  and they're both clearly in love with  her and then you find out that charlie's  actually married  to allison brie's character and michelle  now it's obviously predictable that  mina and charlie were gonna have an  affair here they're showing you that  writing on the wall from the start  but i think it was purposely so obvious  because  that wasn't supposed to be this surprise  shocking moment that was supposed to  just  develop into a big storyline causing  all these suspenseful events to happen  later on with the killer and opening the  door for the killer schemes to play out  and it also serves to the theme of this  killer kind of  being like the jigsaw killer from the  saw films where  he has some method to his madness but  also has some kind of moral belief here  we kind of get hints at  where he doesn't just kill these people  he wants to expose the bad in them and  before he kills  michelle and josh he wants them to see  the truth so  is there some good to it or is it  actually worse getting to see these  awful truths and then you die  so you get a horrible image of humanity  before you go out so maybe where the  predictability  didn't work as well was actually taylor  the man who is selling the rental home  to them  being such a predictable red herring you  knew from the start he was gonna be the  red herring it would have been too easy  and ridiculous if he ended up being the  bad guy here because they were making it  too obvious at that point  if he was so you just knew as a result  that no way is he the bad guy so it was  i wish they kind of made it a little  more get you to think maybe he actually  is the guy that kind of took away little  suspense  but what worked really well here was  establishing sympathy for the characters  of josh and michelle  especially in the beach scene where  they're walking together observing the  behavior of  charlie and mina and what franco does  good is that he shows you a lot of point  of view perspectives from people  and he sits on a lot of people he lets  the camera  stay one take on somebody's reaction  shots he's more about the reactions and  the actual actions in this film  and i think that worked well to get  inside the character's heads he also  puts little drops of humor in there  which comes from his real-life wife  allison bree who plays michelle  you feel for michelle this whole way  through and she's on a different  schedule than everyone else  and she's having a horrible time because  of it they want to do drugs when she  doesn't and then when she want to do  these drugs  everyone's just out of it another good  humorous moment here is when they  actually first take the drugs and  charlie's dancing with the dog i thought  that was hysterical  and franco really does a good job with  this fog here  starting to add the eeriness to it and  really good staging  and blocking in this scene where we have  this big moment in the hot tub with mina  and  charlie where there's actually just  suspense where you know the cheating is  going to happen  it's a matter of when and they do a good  job with them first just trading using  the jets and then sharing it and then  leading up to this big betrayal  and reggie the dog is so important here  he's not just there to be cute he's a  huge part of the symbolism in this film  that happens because right when the  affair happens in the hot tub he starts  to bark  it's just showing you that that reggie  here is  the symbolism of innocence and is lost  at that moment in our group  and you see the next day after the  affair this is when reggie goes missing  and why a little shot of reggie just  playing with the football is so  important  the next day to then seeing him gone  that little innocent figure  and it pays off in the end because my  favorite moment of the film is actually  at the end when reggie comes back and  he's with the killer and the killer  doesn't kill him he never killed the dog  and he didn't want to in the end because  he was this little innocent guy thought  that was a huge  effective chilling scene and thought out  well and he  purposely selected his point of view  shots well from the killer  it actually is first hinted at when we  see an overhead shot of michelle in bed  and then the big one is when it's really  obvious it's the killer here watching  them  is when he's watching them at the hot  tub having this affair  so his point of view shots are important  because they're not just him scouting  out when he's gonna kill these people  it's him scouting out the right timing  and catching them in their worst moments  that goes to when michelle goes on the  hike with josh and michelle finds out  more about  her husband she didn't know that he's  pretty much a serial cheater  and that hits on that theme where  sometimes we date someone  when they're with somebody else and you  think you're the special one and it's  not going to happen to you but  most of the time people repeat their  behavior and that's what starts to scare  her about her husband and we see  charlie falls into that most unlikable  person here in the movie he is a serial  liar besides just being a serial cheater  when mina finds the camera in the shower  she has a smart decision so just  let's call the cops do the right thing  and  charlie's first reaction is no because  then they're gonna find out about the  affair  so he puts that first it's a selfish  decision that just leads to more chaos  and just lets his killer schemes work  into his play  meena's just as guilty as charlie here  and they're both  fine with covering up the affair even  though  they know doing that is having michelle  and josh be spied on when they're  showering that day it's creepy  and i mean allison brie absolutely kills  it here with the humor i thought another  great scene was her  just when she's on the molly just  teasing taylor with his butt crack that  was a really nice moment it adds relief  throughout this  and it makes you like her more and more  so you feel worse for her as this goes  on where she's so out of the loop and  you see where mina really screws up is  actually where she accuses  taylor of completely being convinced  he's the one who put the camera up there  when he was actually starting to be nice  to her he's saying that he hopes she  actually finds her dog and she kind of  just  lets anger run her better judgment here  and that just starts  a chain of effects where josh thinks  taylor's actually hurting mina  and then his anger comes out that was  hinted at the whole film but you feel  for josh still because he didn't  actually kill this guy and he's  defending mina  thinking something else is going on but  mina's not telling josh the whole  truth here and then he gets screwed over  because this perfectly works to the  killer  where it's cleverly written that the  killer actually goes and  kills taylor himself to really frame  josh and cause even more chaos between  these four people and a big moment  for characters with michelle and charlie  is how opposite they are in the horror  she's realizing more and more about her  husband  how much of really a selfish lying  person he is  and her saying let's call the cops  and he's like no we can get away with  this protect our brother from going to  jail  but michelle's the most genuine here and  she's like i know we can get away with  it i don't want to get away with it i  don't want to live with that but you see  mina and charlie the way they're able to  hold their affair  and hold on to that without saying  anything is the same way they're  ready to lie about this murder so it  does show you that difference in  characters and it works really well  i also really like how franco revealed  to michelle's character the shower  footage  that as michelle were thinking the  shower's on  in one shower and then she checks the  other shower but then she finds the  footage on the tv i thought that was  really clever  and once she sees this footage this is  where we first see the killer's  masked face and the michael myers show  begins i thought it was  funny the scene where they're kind of  throwing this  rock to push taylor into the ocean  because they messed up on the original  throw of his body  but it dragged a little bit here for the  sake of humor that maybe  wasn't even necessary and that would be  my only critique in the debut  of franco's film is i think his pacing  is a little  wonky and can get a little more tighter  and pick up a little quicker  a lot of thrillers and horrors that come  out now like doing this thing where  halfway through in the movie things pick  up and that's fine you want to earn to  get to that  quicker pace and build up your  characters but i do think  still really slow up parts before that  and they gotta wind it up a little  earlier but that's me nitpicking and  that's not something that i think was  too detrimental to the film i do wish  having to wait for big payoff with the  kills you want clever kills in an  audience you've seen a lot of slashers  before you've seen people chasing people  down so you want the big payoff and the  actual killing  i only think we got one of those which  was with mina i thought it was  really cool how she ended up dying  running off the cliff  and threw the fog so it spoke volumes it  connected to her character well  and it was a certain karma to it that's  why i wish charlie who's the most  unlikable hero got the best death and it  was just him getting hit by a hammer  as the other two characters did as well  so that was a little  bland and could have been more creative  and there was a weird head-scratching  moment  that was just strange to me i did not  understand was when  josh actually gets through this door  with mina  and they're looking through the boxes  and josh just finds these pictures  and we as the audience don't see them  clearly we don't really know what he saw  there  i mean is like what are those and he's  like jesus you don't want to know  okay that's a weird line to throw in  there because we never get an answer on  that what was the point of that that was  strange and why wouldn't he tell mina  but worked effectively emotionally in  the scene was josh hugging her saying  he's a piece of crap to meena  and you feel for josh again because this  is a big moment mina could have been  honest with him about the affair and  maybe  made him feel a little better about  himself but she doesn't and he doesn't  know about the affair which is a vital  info to all this and he doesn't know  that he actually didn't kill  taylor so it works to serve that purpose  of this  theme about how actions of just two  people affect the whole group and bad  decisions  and that mina and charlie just keep  repeating these mistakes of lying so  after  the killer ends up killing the four main  players here  i like the creepy moment of him looking  in the mirror with his mask on and like  i said before my favorite moment was a  dog running into the killer  clearly franco did not want to show you  the face of the killer because he wants  to keep that eeriness  and truth to it that killers like this  that  just peeping tom people and  kill them from afar you never see their  face so he wants to keep that effect to  make you think about it  later on after watching the film so i  think that was actually a smart choice  might frustrate some viewers though  because in movies like this you kind of  want some kind of twist or explanation  of to who this killer was so it's a  tough sell but i understand it to work  with the theme of this movie  and franco clearly wants to show in this  last montage  when we're seeing him go to different  rental places the killer how easy it is  for him to set something like this up  and how it can happen so much  and just a warning to us as an audience  to really be careful  trusting strangers to be the people  housing you so to me this is a really  excellent directorial debut  it feels like if you look at great  directors in the past who've made great  films it feels like a lot of their  debuts in the sense of there's a lot of  potential here  for some greatness to come in his future  films i think that's gonna happen  i think this is really really good for a  first film it's a solid thriller it has  great acting the performances shine  and it has some really good suspense in  which you want a thriller towards the  end  i just think the pacing was a little off  and that could be worked on in his  future films but thematically it sets a  goal  it accomplished its goal and you're  invested the whole time and it really is  few and far between with thrillers and  horrors right now to have such  an edge of your seat ending so i thought  that worked really well so i'm giving  this one an  8.7 let me know what you thought of it  down below i'd love to hear your  thoughts  also i got to interview jeremy allen  white himself  who plays josh in this film and you  probably know him as lip gallagher from  shameless it was a really fun  chat i got with him so please check that  out be linked below and please make sure  to subscribe so you don't miss one of my  reviews or celebrity interviews  and please follow me at stevebarley1 on  twitter instagram for more of me and  i'll see you next time


